BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f18aa85c00c3393@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Consent judgment orders California-based home
  appliance company to pay $800K to 339 employees denied overtime pay
DESCRIPTION:LOS ANGELES – The U.S. Department of Labor has obtained a c
 onsent judgment requiring a home appliance company to pay $800\,000 in bac
 k wages and damages to 339 employees after an investigation found the comp
 any violated federal wage laws.The judgment comes after the department’s
  Wage and Hour Division investigated Coway USA Inc. and found that the emp
 loyer paid employees on a piece-rate basis and failed to pay employees for
  time spent attending mandatory meetings\, picking up and loading supplies
 \, and performing work before and after their shifts\, in violation of the
  Fair Labor Standards Act. Investigators also found that Coway did not inc
 lude nondiscretionary incentives\, bonuses\, and commissions in overtime p
 ay calculations and frequently reduced the number of hours employees recor
 ded working on their timecards.Entered in the U.S. District Court for the 
 Central District of California on Sept. 9\, the consent judgment requires 
 Coway to record all hours the implicated employees worked\, including time
  they spend picking up supplies\, attending mandatory training\, and commu
 nicating with customers before and after appointments. Coway is also requi
 red to give employees pay statements that show their hours worked\, servic
 es provided to customers\, and the rate Coway paid for the hours and servi
 ces. The consent judgment orders Coway USA Inc. to pay $400\,000 in unpai
 d overtime wages and an equal amount in damages to 339 employees working i
 n the greater metro areas of Los Angeles\, New York City\, and Washington\
 , D.C. The company also agreed to pay $10\,000 in penalties for willful vi
 olations. Coway USA Inc. is a subsidiary of Coway Co. Ltd.\, a multinatio
 nal company specializing in home appliances. The company installs and main
 tains various appliances\, including water and air filtration products thr
 oughout the U.S.Employers and employees can contact the Wage and Hour Divi
 sion with questions and requests for compliance assistance at its toll-fre
 e helpline\, 866-4US-WAGE (487-9243). Emp\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/rel
 eases/whd/whd20261019
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:72a48630a55cffee@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261019T222103Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261019
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] US Department of Labor announces field assist
 ance bulletin withdrawing 3 opinion letters on tipped employees
DESCRIPTION:WASHINGTON – The U.S. Department of Labor’s Wage and Hour
  Division today announced the issuance of a new field assistance bulletin 
 addressing the treatment of related duties performed by tipped employees u
 nder the Fair Labor Standards Act. Field Assistance Bulletin No. 2026-1 
 brings the division’s guidance on tipped employees with dual jobs into a
 lignment with current regulations.The bulletin withdraws the division’s 
 1979\, 1980\, and 1985 opinion letters concerning the application of dual 
 jobs regulations to tipped employees. The withdrawal of the three opinion 
 letters is necessary because they no longer reflect the division’s polic
 y or interpretation of the FLSA. The bulletin also clarifies that the curr
 ent dual jobs regulation focuses on whether an employee performs duties re
 lated to the employee’s tipped occupation\, rather than imposing a speci
 fic percentage-based limit on the time an employee may spend performing re
 lated\, non-tipped duties within that occupation.The publication of the ne
 w bulletin follows a 2024 federal court decision that vacated the departme
 nt’s 2021 dual jobs final rule. After that decision\, the department rem
 oved the regulatory text adopted under the 2021 rule and reinstated the or
 iginal dual jobs regulations.The new field assistance bulletin clarifies t
 hat no current Wage and Hour Division guidance relies on the former percen
 tage-based limit on the time a tipped employee may spend performing relate
 d\, non-tipped duties\, commonly known as the 80/20 rule. The division rem
 oved its 80/20 rule guidance from its Field Operations Handbook in Januar
 y 2025\, and the handbook is now consistent with the reinstated 1967 dual 
 jobs regulations.As noted in the most recent regulatory agenda\, the depa
 rtment is developing a notice of proposed rulemaking to amend various regu
 lations for tipped employees under the FLSA. The department welcomes publi
 c comments on the proposed rule once it is published.Learn more about the 
 Wage and Hour Division\, including the agen\nhttp://www.dol.gov/newsroom/
 releases/whd/whd20261019-0
URL:http://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20261019-0
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
